            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest notable news is that NASA’s **Perseverance rover has found evidence that ancient rocks on the rim of Jezero Crater were built up by repeated asteroid impacts**. NASA/JPL reported on July 15, 2026, that a 245-foot-thick / 75-meter-thick stack of layered bedrock, called the **“Broom Point member,”** is likely **more than 3.9 billion years old**, making it among the oldest terrain ever examined by a Mars rover. Scientists say these rocks preserve a rare record of early solar-system bombardment that Earth has largely lost because of plate tectonics【5:1†source】.\n\nAnother recent milestone: **Perseverance has driven a full marathon distance on Mars**—26.2 miles / 42.195 km. It reached that mark on its **1,890th Martian day**, after about **five years and four months** of driving, much faster than Opportunity, which took over 11 years to cover the same distance【5:0†source】. NASA’s Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter also captured Perseverance and its tracks from orbit as it traveled west of Jezero Crater in a region nicknamed **“Arbot”**【5:0†source】.\n\nNASA’s Perseverance news feed also shows several related recent updates, including new views of **Broom Point**, maps of the rover’s trip there, and imagery from its push west beyond Jezero Crater【5:3†source】.",
